Z||000010||spike||01-24-2000||09:00 AM||||When I had my 87 Fiero GT about 2 years ago I built a custom box and equiped it with 2 Kenwood 10 inch subs. The box itself was a little to small for the required specs for these subs. I made two small ports in the box to give it more air, but to tell you the truth they sounded better with the ports blocked off. The bass was quite good and the complete system costed about 600$ CDN. I removed the rear speakers and kept only the front ones. Complete system was stock radio, stock front speakers, 200 RMS Amp and 2 Kenwood 10 inch subs. When I get my new Fiero GT this spring I am going to build a slightly larger box to fit 2 12 inchers. I now it is possible due to the space I had left after the 10 inch custom box was installed. Again with no loss of seat travel or leg room at all. This summer my custom box will be on the market. If you love BIG ASS BASS this is the way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula||01-24-2000||01:15 PM||||You really don't need to port it into the cab.  It sounds fine just sitting in there.||||reg||1
Z||000012||spike||01-24-2000||01:35 PM||||I took a look at the website mentioned and I still think my set-up is much better. I like keeping my subs inside the vehicle and in sight. Subs look so good and the way I mount them they are visable from the outside of the vehicle. And as I said the box looks right at home with the matching gray vynle. My box fits so tight no screws or bolts are needed to hold it in place. Cant wait to build my new box with 2 12 inchers. Maybe 4 8 inchers.||||reg||1
